WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.900
That distracts the learner from
the main goal, which is to get

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:05.730
lots of exposure to the language.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.560
Steve Kaufmann here and today
I want to talk about grammar,

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.850
how important is grammar?

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.580
And I'm going to talk about grammar in
the context of all of the details of

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.770
languages that we learned in school.

00:00:20.235 --> 00:00:24.465
How effective is it to teach these
details if the ultimate goal is

00:00:24.465 --> 00:00:26.235
to become fluent in a language?

00:00:26.415 --> 00:00:30.104
Now, remember if you enjoy
these videos, please subscribe.

00:00:30.104 --> 00:00:32.205
You can click on the
bell for notifications.

00:00:32.504 --> 00:00:35.055
By the way, you'll notice that
I have my LingQ t-shirt on.

00:00:35.055 --> 00:00:38.025
I just happened to find that in the closet
when I was getting dressed this morning.

00:00:38.625 --> 00:00:44.535
Um, at any rate, so, you know, details,
one of the things that struck me in

00:00:44.535 --> 00:00:49.980
Manfred Spitzer's book, and I often refer
to it, is the, when he explains that the

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:54.870
brain, our brains and all the brains,
the process of learning is the same.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:59.970
There are no people with brains that
learn better this way or that way,

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:03.629
our brains learn the same, the same
way we have our likes and dislikes.

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:07.590
But the process of learning is the
same and the brains are better...

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:14.009
our brains are better at forming
patterns, recognizing patterns and

00:01:14.009 --> 00:01:15.929
not so good at retaining detail.

00:01:16.995 --> 00:01:20.985
So that, uh, the details that we
learn that we keep somewhere in our

00:01:20.985 --> 00:01:22.665
short-term memory, we lose them.

00:01:22.965 --> 00:01:26.655
But over time, the brain starts
to recognize that pattern.

00:01:27.045 --> 00:01:29.554
And that's very much
in my view, the process

00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:31.205
by which we learn languages.

00:01:31.565 --> 00:01:36.995
So, you know, if you sort of
spell out like grammar, grammar

00:01:36.995 --> 00:01:39.125
basically describes usage.

00:01:39.335 --> 00:01:45.450
So since languages evolve over time,
sounds in languages evolve over time, uh,

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.880
you know, usage patterns evolve over time.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.680
So the grammar which describes
the usage will evolve over time.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:59.400
So how useful is it to attempt to teach
people how the language, what the rules

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:05.430
of usage are before the learner has had
enough experience with the language?

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:06.990
I think it's not that useful.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:12.195
And, at least in our school system
in Canada, where they teach French,

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:16.365
they teach grammar, they teach
grammar because it's something they

00:02:16.365 --> 00:02:18.545
can easily test the learners on.

00:02:18.875 --> 00:02:22.595
And some learners got eight out
of 10 and some got six out of 10.

00:02:22.595 --> 00:02:27.005
So learner A with eight out of 10 is
better than learner B with six out of 10,

00:02:27.305 --> 00:02:32.375
but that doesn't necessarily mean that
at the end of the day learner A is going

00:02:32.375 --> 00:02:35.805
to be better at speaking than learner B.

00:02:35.805 --> 00:02:38.475
It may mean that, but it's not obvious.

00:02:38.924 --> 00:02:44.775
And there are many cases where learner
B who may be exposed more to the

00:02:44.775 --> 00:02:49.335
language, maybe had comes from a, you
know, a country where the language

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:53.205
is spoken hears the language at home,
but he speaks it incorrectly from a

00:02:53.205 --> 00:02:57.215
grammatical point of view and so he does
poorly on the test, but he understands

00:02:57.215 --> 00:02:59.825
and he communicates, and there are
many things, examples like that.

00:03:00.155 --> 00:03:05.375
So my feeling on grammar and other
details of the language is that

00:03:05.405 --> 00:03:07.355
they can be introduced at any time.

00:03:07.755 --> 00:03:10.785
They should be introduced when
the learner is interested in them.

00:03:11.595 --> 00:03:17.115
It's, it's true that many of the patterns
in the language, you know, are not

00:03:17.115 --> 00:03:20.925
noticed when we hear even hearing the
language over and over again, we don't

00:03:20.925 --> 00:03:23.865
notice certain niceties of pronunciation.

00:03:24.495 --> 00:03:27.255
We don't notice that certain
syllables aren't stressed.

00:03:27.615 --> 00:03:32.025
We don't notice, you know, the,
whatever it might be, third person

00:03:32.025 --> 00:03:38.365
singular in English, uh, of the
present tense, uh, it takes an "s".

00:03:38.385 --> 00:03:40.455
Whereas the other forms of the verb don't.

00:03:40.455 --> 00:03:41.325
We don't notice these things.

00:03:41.325 --> 00:03:43.965
So sometimes it can be useful
to point these things out.

00:03:44.295 --> 00:03:48.775
Therefore, I think it's useful to have
a reference grammar [book] and every

00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:52.484
language that I have learned, I have
tried to get a hold of a grammar [book]

00:03:52.995 --> 00:03:58.935
as small as possible with no exercises,
no drills, just what happens in the

00:03:58.935 --> 00:04:01.035
language and a bunch of examples.

00:04:01.215 --> 00:04:03.615
And so I look at that
and I see the examples.

00:04:03.975 --> 00:04:09.735
And it's just helping my brain get
used to how the language works.

00:04:09.825 --> 00:04:14.745
The patterns in the language and
this approach, I think is the same

00:04:14.835 --> 00:04:16.365
for other details of the language.

00:04:16.394 --> 00:04:21.165
For example, pronunciation upfront,
when it is explained to you

00:04:21.165 --> 00:04:25.065
using international phonetic, the
international phonetic alphabet that

00:04:25.065 --> 00:04:26.925
this sound is pronounced this way.

00:04:27.165 --> 00:04:32.820
I don't personally find that very useful,
uh, I find that with the availability of

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:37.170
audio today, text to speech, I can hear
how it's pronounced, but it is possible

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:42.740
that three months later having listened
to the language many, many times, I

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:47.180
still don't notice that a certain vowel
is pronounced a certain way, or I don't

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:52.430
notice that the stress is a certain
way, uh, or in the case of pitch accent

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:54.320
that it's this way rather than that way.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:05:00.090
And so at that time later on to help
us notice things that might've passed

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:05.130
us by it's useful to refer to these
explanations, but there's far too

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:10.590
much emphasis placed, placed on these,
these explanations or these details

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:14.640
of a language, uh, with the intention
of testing people on these details.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:19.110
And the result is normally not very
good because it distracts the learner

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:23.160
from the main goal, which is to get
lots of exposure to the language,

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.719
lots of listening, lots of reading.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:31.330
And as Krashen says, in an environment
that is low stress, that is enjoyable.

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:35.500
And if you do that, and if you
trust your brain, a lot of these

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.539
things will be acquired naturally.

00:05:38.049 --> 00:05:42.340
And therefore you needn't be tested on if
the goal is comprehension, they don't even

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:43.989
need to test you on your comprehension.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.650
Even if you misunderstand something,
the fact that you are putting in

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:51.849
the time, listening and reading
will eventually enable your brain

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:53.140
to get used to the language.

00:05:53.409 --> 00:05:53.919
So, yeah.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:59.659
In my approach to language learning, I
want to sort of, deemphasize the sort

00:05:59.659 --> 00:06:04.460
of details, the grammar, the other
specific explanations, because our brains

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:09.919
are not as good at dealing with these
explanations and rather encourage people

00:06:09.919 --> 00:06:11.810
to find ways to enjoy the language.

00:06:12.255 --> 00:06:13.485
Listen to the language.

00:06:13.515 --> 00:06:15.525
Don't worry about what
you don't understand.

00:06:15.525 --> 00:06:17.535
Don't worry about the
mistakes that you make.

00:06:17.655 --> 00:06:22.545
If you continue to expose yourself to the
language, you will gradually get better.

00:06:22.725 --> 00:06:27.615
And when you are interested in, genuinely
curious about specific details, you can

00:06:27.615 --> 00:06:31.755
look them up and it's never been easier
to look them up, but, but don't get

00:06:31.755 --> 00:06:33.855
yourself a thick grammar book like this.

00:06:34.025 --> 00:06:37.655
Get yourself a thin as possible
grammar book, which explains what's

00:06:37.655 --> 00:06:41.805
happening in the language with
examples, without exercises, without

00:06:41.805 --> 00:06:44.265
drills and go over it regularly.

00:06:44.265 --> 00:06:49.094
Don't try and block learn the
conjugation table of a specific verb.

00:06:49.245 --> 00:06:53.955
Just look it over, forget it, look
it over and recognize you're going

00:06:53.955 --> 00:06:56.175
to forget it and go back there.

00:06:56.205 --> 00:07:01.965
And eventually over time that activity
looking things up, grammar rules,

00:07:01.965 --> 00:07:05.895
whatever, uh, which are limited
unlike the amount of words we have to

00:07:05.895 --> 00:07:08.445
learn, which is almost unlimited, the
grammar rules are actually quite nice.

00:07:09.075 --> 00:07:12.885
And if you keep on flipping through a
sort of a thin grammar book, you will

00:07:12.885 --> 00:07:16.605
eventually get a very good overview
of what happens in the language.

00:07:16.995 --> 00:07:19.965
And when you combine that with lots
of listening and reading, you're

00:07:19.965 --> 00:07:23.355
going to get better and better at
the language rather than trying to

00:07:23.355 --> 00:07:25.995
fight the rules of grammar upfront.

00:07:26.175 --> 00:07:31.725
So that's my take on grammar and I I've
covered this subject before and I'll leave

00:07:31.725 --> 00:07:33.975
a few other videos on the same subject.

00:07:34.815 --> 00:07:35.445
Bye for now.

